{"requirements":[{"id":"mount-hardening","requirement":"Mount /tmp and /dev/shm with noexec, nosuid, and nodev so execution, setuid semantics, and device-node access are ignored.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both final mounts directly show nosuid,nodev,noexec. Before and after reboot, execution and device access probes were denied on both paths; the retained 4755 file mode together with the active nosuid mount flag supports ignored setuid semantics.","evidence_ids":["cmd-34070f58774b47a0","cmd-964aa28764434691","cmd-cf38b7db57144d1c"]},{"id":"reboot-persistence","requirement":"Ensure the hardened mount settings survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Validated fstab entries contain all three hardening flags. A bounded reboot changed the boot ID, the system recovered to running with no failed units, and both mounts retained the required options and passed post-reboot enforcement probes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0dbd59c18af14c78","cmd-9de169388df24cf7","cmd-e6f3b699aeae47d6","cmd-9388f66326a34c22","cmd-9d00fe15bf8b433d","cmd-ce4aa0dfeda14e7b","cmd-643a4e719df04f4f","cmd-cf38b7db57144d1c","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"platform-retention","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS Stream 10 and kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64 are identical before and after execution. The implementation used existing native mount, systemd, and fstab facilities, with package health remaining intact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5b43c241f0624188","cmd-a708cf22be6045d6","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The fstab edit, backup, daemon reload, mounts, relabeling, and reboot were directly connected to implementation, safety, persistence testing, or the mandatory restart protocol. Probe files and device nodes were bounded and removed in both test runs. The retained fstab backup had a clear rollback role. Final observations show no failed services, package damage, new exposure, or executor probe residue; socket differences are only post-reboot process identifiers.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0dbd59c18af14c78","cmd-34070f58774b47a0","cmd-964aa28764434691","cmd-e6f3b699aeae47d6","cmd-cf38b7db57144d1c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Both locations are actively hardened, persistence was demonstrated through a complete reboot and healthy recovery, and the operating-system release and kernel were retained. The provider-wide restart protocol is coherent and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["Because noexec was active, the probes could not separately execute a setuid program to demonstrate privilege suppression; nosuid is instead directly established from the kernel-reported mount options.","The topology contains no separate application, data service, or cluster subsystem to probe during restart recovery; evidence covers system health, expected listeners, persistent configuration, and the hardened mounts."]}
